  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 320 AM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nMCS across north central Nebraska as of 08Z is expected to build \neast with an ever so slight south drift as the 850-300 mb mean wind \nis largely west to east. Atmosphere ahead of this complex is primed \nwith nearly 4000 J/kg of MU CAPE and 35 -45 knots of effective \nshear. The focus of the MCS is largely expected to remain north \nacross Iowa; however, storms developing along the outflow could \nbuild into northern Missouri this morning.  Forecast soundings \nacross northern Missouri show a dry sub cloud layer leading to 1500-\n1700 J/kg of downdraft CAPE suggesting damaging wind potential with \nanything that is able to develop this far south.  \n\nBehind the departing MCS, expect atmosphere to recover with \nsouthwest flow feeding into the region from Texas. With that said, \nsome hi-res CAMs suggest that outflow may linger across the region \npotentially leading to cooler high temperatures than in the forecast \nif this becomes reality. For now, have forecast highs returning into \nthe 90s with dew points in the 70s leading to heat indices of 105-\n110 F across much the area.  At this point, heat headlines look \nreasonable.\n\nLow pressure across western KS is expected to promote easterly winds \nacross Nebraska.  The upslope along with short wave ejecting from \nthe desert southwest is expected to lead to lat afternoon \nthunderstorm development across the western High Plains.  Models are \noffering a variety of solutions on how these storms evolve, but \nnorthern Missouri could get clipped by any complex that develops. \nThe severity would be dependent on how well the atmosphere is able \nto recover throughout the day today.  The convection may also push \nstationary boundary across central Iowa southward, potentially as \nfar as northern Missouri. \n\nLow level jet is expected to develop again from eastern Texas to \nwestern/ central Missouri.  While the dry air just above the surface \nwould limit convective potential, the low level jet will keep the \natmosphere quasi-mixed keeping overnight lows mild once again.  This \nwill set the stage for another mild day Thursday into Friday south \nof the boundary as the long standing low pressure across the western \nHigh Plains slowly drifts north. As the low builds north, it is \nexpected to strengthen, and the associated cold front could be the \nfocus of showers and thunderstorms with the potential for more \nseasonal temperatures returning to the region early next week.",
